An experiment to test whether continual learning through agentic memory engineering
can lead to Domain Superintelligence. Kinda a long shot, but we'll see!

The world runs on its own clock. Time passes, weather happens, crops ripen
and rot whether or not anyone is connected.

Unless you turn that off. A sim set turn_based has no clock of its own and
moves only when someone says "wait", which is the mode to use when a model
is playing: it is deciding what to do, not racing an inference budget, and
a run costs one decision per thing it chooses to do rather than one per
in-game hour.

Two views of the same world
    The agent is a person who has never farmed. It sees only what a person
    standing in the field could see: plants that look small or full grown or
    ripe, soil that looks damp, a plant that looks unwell. It is told nothing
    about water, daylight, thirst or rot. It has to work that out.

    The human gets the instruments: exact hours of growth left, hours until
    a ripe crop spoils, moisture level, thirst counter.

    Pass ?view=human on the read routes for the instrumented view. Everything
    under "Rules" below is knowledge the agent does not start with -- keep it
    out of any system prompt you hand the model.

Many worlds
    "main" is the shared sim, at /. It is the one the agent lives in, it
    never expires, and visitors cannot reconfigure it.

    Drag any slider in the sidebar and a "start your own sim" button appears.
    That forks a private world with those settings, running its own clock,
    which you can retune and restart as often as you like without touching
    main. "back to the main sim" returns you.

    Every play and read route takes ?world=<id>, defaulting to main.

Every sim has a URL
    / is the main sim and nothing else. Every other sim has its own path,
    and the browser is sent there rather than staying put:

        fork a sim   -> /<hash>   an unguessable 8-character id, /2ec59022
        name it      -> /<name>   a public sim, /my-farm
        back to main -> /

    The URL is the sim. Changing world is a real navigation, not a rewritten
    address bar, so a reload, a bookmark, a shared link and the back button
    all land on the sim you were actually in. /main redirects to /.

    Names are 2-24 characters of a-z, 0-9 and dashes, lowercased, and unique
    across every sim. At most 64 public sims exist.

    Publishing moves the sim: the hash URL stops working and anyone still
    watching it gets {"world", "moved": <name>} and is sent to the new one.
    There is no ownership -- a public sim is as editable as main, so the
    admin key is the only thing between a named sim and a stranger
    restarting it.

Public sims are permanent, private ones expire
    A private sim closes 5 minutes after the last thing that actually changed
    it. Only a delta counts: planting, watering, harvesting, a restart, a
    settings change. Looking, streaming and failed commands do not, so an
    abandoned tab cannot hold a world open forever. At most 24 exist at once.

    Publishing a sim cancels that clock for good. main is simply the public
    sim that ships with the server.

    A sweep runs every 5 seconds, independent of any world's clock speed, so
    a sim set to one tick per hour still expires on time. From 60 seconds out
    the server pushes a countdown on that world's stream and the page raises
    a modal: keep the sim open, or go back to main. At zero the world is
    dropped, subscribers get {"world", "gone": true}, and the stream closes.

    POST /api/keepalive?world=<id> resets the 5 minutes without changing
    anything. main never expires and always reports expires_in: null.

Run
    pip install -r requirements.txt
    uvicorn main:app
    open http://localhost:8000

    TOKENFARM_SECONDS_PER_HOUR=5   starting clock speed for main
    TOKENFARM_TURN_BASED=true      start main with no clock of its own, for
                                   pointing a single agent at the server
    TOKENFARM_ADMIN_KEY=...        locks config and sim-forking behind
                                   X-Admin-Key. Unset means anyone who can
                                   reach the server can read the physics --
                                   set it if the model has raw network access.
                                   The sidebar has a field for it.

Files
    farm.py     config, simulation, hourly tick, commands, both views
    main.py     FastAPI app: one clock task per world, 10 routes
    db.py       sqlite storage, one row per world, keyed by hash or name
    index.html  ascii view, live log, command bar, slider sidebar
    STYLE.md    how to write code here. Read it before editing.
                General-purpose; copy it into any repo.

Persistence
    Every world is written to data/tokenfarm.db on every tick and every
    command that changes something, and reloaded from there on startup -- a
    restart resumes every sim exactly where it left off, including the
    weather RNG and whether it is public. A private sim's 5-minute expiry
    clock is not persisted, so one that survives a restart gets a fresh
    countdown. A sim dropped by the janitor is deleted from the db in the
    same sweep, as is the old hash row when a sim is published.

Settings
    pace        seconds_per_hour   real seconds per in-game hour
                seed               blank for random weather, an int to repeat
                turn_based         no clock of its own; time moves only on
                                   "wait". seconds_per_hour is then unused

    world       rows, cols         size of the field
                start_money        opening balance
                crops              {name: {cost, hours, price}}. Names must
                                   start with different letters.

    physics     dawn, dusk         when daylight starts and ends
                water_hours        how long one watering lasts
                thirst_hours       dry hours a crop survives
                rot_hours          how long a ripe crop keeps
                rain_chance        chance rain starts, per hour
                rain_stops         chance rain stops, per hour

    perception  stages             how finely the agent can see growth, 1-5.
                                   1 means a sprout and a nearly ripe plant
                                   look identical
                show_distress      whether wilting is visible at all. Off and
                                   death arrives with no warning

    control     spoil_rules        hands the agent the instrumented view and
                                   the full rulebook. Off by default; it
                                   exists so you can measure discovery
                                   against being told

API
    GET  /                            -> the page, on main
    GET  /<hash|name>                 -> the page, on that sim. /main is a
                                         307 back to /; a malformed id 404s
    GET  /api/state?world=&view=      -> state
    GET  /api/events?world=&since=    -> {events, cursor}  what you missed
    GET  /api/stream?world=&view=     -> SSE {world, events, state} per tick
    POST /api/command?world=&view=    -> {ok, message, events, state}
                     {"text": "..."}
    GET  /api/config?world=           -> {world, shared, public, expires_in,
                                          config, limits, rules}
    POST /api/worlds {settings}       -> same, for a brand new private sim
    POST /api/config?world= {settings}-> same, applied to that sim, restarted
    POST /api/restart?world= {}       -> same, same settings, from day 1
    POST /api/keepalive?world= {}     -> same, session clock reset
    POST /api/publish?world=          -> same, under the new id. 400 on a bad
                     {"name": "..."}     name or an already-public sim, 409 if
                                         the name is taken

    The last six need X-Admin-Key when TOKENFARM_ADMIN_KEY is set. The play
    routes never do.

    Stream frames are {world, events, state, expires_in}. A countdown frame
    carries only {world, expires_in}; the last frame of a dying world is
    {world, gone: true}, and of a published one {world, moved: <name>}.

    state = {day, time, phase, weather, money, barn, crops, plots, ascii,
             cursor, revision}
    plots, agent view = {"A1": {crop, looks, damp}, ...}
    plots, human view = {"A1": {crop, state, hours_left, rots_in, moisture,
                                thirst}, ...}

    cursor is the id of the newest event; keep it between turns and pass it
    to /api/events to replay exactly what was missed. It drops to 0 on a
    restart, which is how a client detects a new world. revision changes on
    any mutation, so an agent can tell "nothing happened" cheaply.

    Events are observations, never explanations: "A3 shrivelled up", not
    "A3 died of thirst". Both views share them.

Commands (send "help" for the in-game version)
    look | till <plots> | plant <crop> <plots> | water <plots>
    harvest <plots> | clear <plots> | sell <crop|all> [n] | log [n]
    wait [n]                                  turn_based sims only

    <plots> accepts a cell (a1), a rectangle (a1-b3), "all", or a mix.

    "wait" lets n hours pass, 1 by default and 168 at most, and is the only
    way time moves in a turn_based sim. On a sim with its own clock it is
    refused -- nobody can fast-forward a world other people are watching --
    and "help" does not list it there. Call sim.tick(farm) directly to drive
    any world synchronously in tests.

    Choosing when to wake up is part of the game: an agent that waters at
    dusk and sleeps until dawn spends nothing on the hours in between.

    "look" always renders the farmer's-eye view, so you can see what the
    model sees. The browser pane is the instrumented one.

Grid
    agent    .  bare   -  tilled   x  dead   ~  damp   !  looks unwell
             w1 w2 w3  sprout / half grown / nearly full grown
             W         looks ripe

    human    w14  14 growing hours left      W46  ripe, spoils in 46h
             ~    damp                       !5   5 hours thirsty

Rules -- SPOILERS, this is what the agent must discover
    Defaults below; the sidebar prints the live version, generated from the
    running config, so it is never stale.

    06:00 dawn, 20:00 dusk. In daylight crops grow one hour per hour on moist
    soil, and soil dries one hour per hour. At night everything pauses, so
    water poured at dusk is still there at dawn. Rain soaks every tilled plot
    at any hour. A crop dies after 20 thirsty hours, and a ripe crop rots 48
    hours after it ripens -- an absent farmer loses the harvest. A plant shows
    "!" halfway to either death, so trouble is visible before it is fatal.

    Crops: wheat $5/20h/$12, corn $12/40h/$30, tomato $20/60h/$55,
    pumpkin $40/100h/$120. Hours are daylight growing hours.
    Start with $50. Money is the score.

    Seed and sale prices are posted on the seed board, visible to the agent.
    Prices are written on signs; biology is not. To hide prices too, drop
    "price" from the agent branch of _seen/snapshot in farm.py.
